Let all of libkern/crypto fail instead of faulting when corecrypto is absent.

This replaces aes-without-corecrypto.patch, which covered one file. Its own
closing note said what would happen next:

    The rest of libkern/crypto -- SHA, MD5, HMAC, ChaCha20 -- has the
    identical shape and will fault the same way when something reaches it.

Something did. With HFS mounting the RAM disk, the boot now dies in

    pc  _MD5Init + 0x34
    lr  _hfs_getvoluuid + 0x5c
    esr 0x96000005      far 0x0000000000000020

far 0x20 is the offset of ccmd5_di in struct crypto_functions: four digest
function pointers, then the digest infos. The same count puts
ccaes_cbc_encrypt at 0x78, which is where the earlier AES fault landed. One
absence, two symptoms.

g_crypto_funcs is filled in by register_crypto_functions(), whose only caller
is the corecrypto kext. This kernel has no kexts, so it stays NULL, and every
wrapper in this directory opens by reading a field out of it.

So the whole directory is covered at once rather than a file per boot. The
shape is the one Apple already uses for the GCM modes -- read through a macro
that yields NULL when the table is missing, then check:

    #define CRYPTO_MODE(field)  (g_crypto_funcs ? g_crypto_funcs->field : NULL)

File by file:

  corecrypto_aes.c    unchanged from the earlier patch: 22 mode reads routed
                      through CRYPTO_MODE, and the four CBC wrappers given the
                      check the GCM ones already had.

  corecrypto_md5.c    MD5Init, MD5Update, MD5Final.
  corecrypto_sha1.c   SHA1Init, SHA1Update, SHA1Final.
  corecrypto_sha2.c   SHA{256,384,512}_{Init,Update,Final}.

                      These return void, so there is no way to report the
                      failure. They do nothing instead, and leave something
                      deterministic: Init zeroes the context, Final zeroes the
                      digest. Without that, a caller reads whatever was on the
                      stack and cannot tell it from a hash.

  corecrypto_des.c    The two key schedules return -1; the two single-block
                      encrypt entry points return void and zero their output
                      block. des_is_weak_key() returns 0, which nothing can
                      act on because the key schedule has already refused.

  corecrypto_rsa.c    Both entry points return -1. rsa_verify_pkcs1v15() also
                      clears *valid, which its callers would otherwise read
                      uninitialised from the stack -- on the code-signing
                      path, that is the difference between a failed check and
                      an accidentally passed one.

  corecrypto_chacha20poly1305.c
                      Every entry point here is "return fns()->x(info(), ...)",
                      so each gets its own check; making fns() return NULL
                      alone would move the dereference one line down.

corecrypto_aesxts.c is deliberately left alone. xts_start() already opens with

    if (!g_crypto_funcs) {
            panic("%s: corecrypto not registered!\n", __FUNCTION__);
    }

so it says what is wrong instead of faulting, and xts_encrypt/xts_decrypt
cannot be reached without a key schedule from it. XTS is FileVault and
CoreStorage; neither exists here.

What this does not do is make any of it work. A digest now returns zeros
rather than faulting, and that is enough for hfs_getvoluuid(), which only
wants a volume UUID. It is not enough for code signing: iOS validates every
binary against a SHA hash and an RSA signature through this same table, so a
real iOS userland needs corecrypto built into the kernel, the way libpthread
and HFS now are. These guards are not a substitute for that; they are what
keeps the kernel alive until it is done, and they stay correct afterwards --
once something registers the table, every check here passes.

